<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui">
    <!--
        Popups render in their own host (a PopupRoot top-level on desktop), so they are not visual
        descendants of the content SukiMainHost scales through its LayoutTransformControl. Avalonia
        only propagates that scale when Popup.InheritsTransform is true, and it defaults to false:
        Popup.UpdateHostSizing() then assigns `popupHost.Transform = null` and the popup renders at
        1:1 while the window content around it is scaled.

        ContextMenu, MenuFlyout/Flyout and ToolTip build their Popup in code (ContextMenu.Open,
        PopupFlyoutBase.CreatePopup, ToolTip) with a private field and no virtual factory, so there
        is no XAML surface to set the flag on. A style reaches them because those popups are given a
        logical parent (SetPopupParent), which is what style resolution walks.

        ShouldUseOverlayLayer=False is required, not cosmetic: OverlayLayer resolves to the nearest
        VisualLayerManager, and SukiMainHost nests PART_VisualLayerManager *inside* its
        LayoutTransformControl. An overlay-hosted popup is therefore already a descendant of the
        scale, and handing it a Transform as well would square it.

        Caveat: this does not cover platform-forced overlay hosting (OverlayPopupHost.CreatePopupHost
        also falls back to the overlay layer when the platform has no popup implementation), so on
        such targets popups still take the scale twice. Fixing that properly belongs upstream, where
        Popup can see which host it actually got.
    -->
    <Style Selector="Popup[ShouldUseOverlayLayer=False]">
        <Setter Property="InheritsTransform" Value="True" />
    </Style>
</Styles>
